analysis.reasoning:
The bug is confirmed by tracing the referer handling flow. When `curl_easy_setopt(easy, CURLOPT_REFERER, NULL)` is called, it sets `data->set.str[STRING_SET_REFERER] = NULL` via `Curl_setstropt` (lib/setopt.c:2026). However, in `Curl_pretransfer` (lib/transfer.c:509-513), the code only updates `data->state.referer` when `data->set.str[STRING_SET_REFERER]` is non-NULL:

```c
if(data->set.http_auto_referer)
  Curl_bufref_free(&data->state.referer);
if(data->set.str[STRING_SET_REFERER])
  Curl_bufref_set(&data->state.referer, data->set.str[STRING_SET_REFERER], 0, NULL);
```

Since the second `if` is skipped when the user explicitly clears the referer, `data->state.referer` retains its previous pointer (and length/dtor). The next `curl_easy_perform` calls `Curl_pretransfer` again (which doesn't clear it) and then `http_add_hd` for `H1_HD_REFERER` (lib/http.c:2931-2937) reads `Curl_bufref_ptr(&data->state.referer)` and emits the stale Referer header. `CURLINFO_REFERER` (lib/getinfo.c:146-149) also returns this stale pointer. The ASan UAF occurs because `Curl_bufref_free` may be called elsewhere (e.g., during redirect handling or cleanup) on the same `bufref`, freeing the memory while the pointer remains in `data->state.referer`.

